    <h2>Context and Objectives</h2>
    <p>
      This project was carried out as part of my upskilling in system and network administration. The goal was to deploy a complete and functional IP telephony infrastructure (IPBX), then go further by integrating an external notification system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Traditional telephony solutions are often expensive and closed. <strong>Issabel</strong> (based on Asterisk) is a powerful open-source alternative that allows understanding the inner workings of VoIP. The addition of the <strong>Telegram</strong> integration aimed to automate supervision: never miss an important call, even outside the office.
    </p>
    <p>
      This project was carried out solo over a period of 3 days. It required a research phase, installation, network configuration, diagnostics and development.
    </p>

    <h2>Technical Architecture</h2>
    <p>The system relies on a clear separation between network infrastructure and automation:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Issabel 5 (Asterisk 20)</strong>: Core of the system, hosted on a Rocky Linux VM. It handles SIP registrations and call routing.</li>
      <li><strong>Zoiper Clients</strong>: Softphones installed on PC and mobile, configured to use the modern PJSIP driver over UDP.</li>
      <li><strong>Bash Script</strong>: Located on the server, it interacts with the Telegram HTTP API to send formatted alerts.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom Dialplan</strong>: Asterisk's <code>extensions_custom.conf</code> file was modified to create a context capable of executing the Bash script via the <code>System()</code> application.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Phase 1: VM Deployment and Network Configuration</h2>
    <p>
      The first step was to create a virtual machine under VMware Workstation. I chose <strong>Rocky Linux</strong> as the guest operating system, as it is the base for Issabel 5. The VM was configured with 4 GB RAM and 2 vCPU.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first challenge was the network. By default, VMware uses <strong>NAT</strong> mode, which isolates the VM on a private network (192.168.80.x). For Zoiper clients (on the host PC and mobile) to reach the server, I had to switch the VM to <strong>Bridged</strong> mode. This allows the VM to obtain an IP address on the physical network, like any other device.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, on the first boot, the VM failed to obtain a DHCP lease. I had to intervene manually:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Check interface status with <code>ip a</code>.</li>
      <li>Attempt to restart the connection with <code>sudo nmcli connection up eth0</code> (failed).</li>
      <li>Restart the network service with <code>sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager</code> (failed).</li>
      <li>Use the <code>sudo nmtui</code> tool to manually configure a static IP address: <code>192.168.1.150/24</code>, gateway <code>192.168.1.254</code>, DNS <code>8.8.8.8</code>.</li>
      <li>Final verification with <code>ip a</code> and connectivity test with <code>ping 192.168.1.254</code> and <code>ping 192.168.1.64</code> (the host PC).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Once the network was operational, I could access the Issabel web interface from my browser at <code>https://192.168.1.150</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 2: Issabel Installation and Configuration</h2>
    <p>
      Issabel 5 was installed via the official ISO. The process is standard (language, keyboard, disk, root password). After reboot, I accessed the web administration interface.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first task was to create the SIP extensions. I chose the <strong>PJSIP</strong> driver (modern) rather than the old <code>chan_sip</code>. I created two extensions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1001</strong>: for the Zoiper client on PC.</li>
      <li><strong>1003</strong>: for the Zoiper client on mobile.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each extension was configured with a secret (password) and a voicemail context. Once the extensions were created, I clicked "Apply Config" to reload the Asterisk configuration.
    </p>
    <p>
      Endpoint status verification with <code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show endpoints"</code>. The extensions were listed, but their status was <code>Unavailable</code> because the Zoiper clients were not yet configured.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 3: Zoiper Client Configuration and TCP/UDP Conflict</h2>
    <p>
      I installed Zoiper on my PC and smartphone. The initial configuration required:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Username: <code>1001</code> (or <code>1003</code>)</li>
      <li>Password: the secret defined in Issabel</li>
      <li>Domain: <code>192.168.1.150</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The clients registered successfully (green light in Zoiper). However, calls consistently failed with a <code>503 Service Unavailable</code> error.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is where the major technical challenge of the project came in. By analyzing Asterisk logs in real time (<code>sudo asterisk -rvvv</code>), I discovered that Zoiper was using the <strong>TCP</strong> protocol while the server expected <strong>UDP</strong>. The exact error was:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{asteriskLog}</code></pre>
    <p>
      To resolve this issue, I had to force the transport to UDP in Zoiper's settings. Then, I cleared the old TCP registration with <code>pjsip send unregister 1003</code>, then reloaded the configuration with <code>pjsip reload</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Final verification with <code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show endpoints"</code>: the status changed from <code>Unavailable</code> to <code>Reachable</code>. Calls could finally go through.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 4: Bash Script Development for Telegram</h2>
    <p>
      The goal was to send a Telegram notification on every unanswered call. To do this, I first created a Telegram bot via <strong>@BotFather</strong>. I obtained an API token and a chat ID.
    </p>
    <p>
      I then developed a Bash script (<code>/usr/local/bin/send_telegram_alert.sh</code>) that uses <code>curl</code> to send an HTTP POST request to the Telegram API:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{bashScript}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Successful manual test: <code>sudo /usr/local/bin/send_telegram_alert.sh "1001" "1003"</code>. The notification arrived on my smartphone.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 5: Asterisk Dialplan Integration</h2>
    <p>
      To link a phone call to a system action, I created a custom context in <code>/etc/asterisk/extensions_custom.conf</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{dialplanConfig}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then, in the Issabel web interface, I created a <strong>Custom Destination</strong> pointing to <code>telegram-alert,s,1</code> and assigned it to the "Unavailable" field of extension 1003.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, the Issabel interface refused to accept the custom destination format. I had to work around the problem by creating a dummy code (<code>9999</code>) in <code>from-internal-custom</code> to force redirection to the script.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dialplan reload with <code>sudo asterisk -rx "dialplan reload"</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 6: Testing and Validation</h2>
    <p>
      The testing phase consisted of simulating an unanswered call from 1001 to 1003. I verified in the Asterisk console (<code>sudo asterisk -rvvv</code>) that the message <code>--- TELEGRAM SCRIPT LAUNCH ---</code> appeared. And the Telegram notification arrived on my smartphone.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also tested the voicemail by calling <code>*97</code> from Zoiper. The voicemail was recorded and the Telegram alert was sent if the extension was "Unavailable".
    </p>

    <h2>Video demonstrations</h2>
    <p>Here are the video demonstrations of the project in action:</p>
    
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mt-8">
      {videos.map(({ key, url }) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass" style="border-color: var(--glass-border);">
          <video controls class="w-full" preload="metadata">
            <source src={url} type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser does not support video playback.
          </video>
          <div class="p-3">
            <p class="font-mono text-[10px]" style="color: var(--text-muted);">{key}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2>Final Result</h2>
    <p>The project is <strong>functional</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>✅ Operational Issabel 5 server on VMware VM (Rocky Linux)</li>
      <li>✅ PJSIP extensions configured and Zoiper clients registered in UDP</li>
      <li>✅ Bash script correctly executed by Asterisk on each unanswered call</li>
      <li>✅ Telegram notification instantly received on smartphone</li>
      <li>✅ Stable and documented system</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Future Improvements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Telegram Call Gateway (tg2sip)</strong> — To allow making and receiving voice calls directly from Telegram, in addition to notifications.</li>
      <li><strong>Enriched Notifications</strong> — Add the caller's name (if known), call time and a direct link to the supervision panel.</li>
      <li><strong>Advanced Supervision</strong> — Integration of a Grafana dashboard to visualize line status and call history in real time.</li>
      <li><strong>High Availability</strong> — Setup of an Asterisk cluster with database replication to avoid single points of failure.</li>
      <li><strong>Security</strong> — Implementation of TLS/SRTP to encrypt SIP and RTP communications, and IP-based access restriction.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Appendix: Exhaustive Log of Commands Used</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the complete list of commands used, categorized by phase. Each command was used to solve a specific problem.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Network Diagnostics and Configuration</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>ip a</code>: Displays the status of network interfaces and IP addresses.</li>
      <li><code>sudo nmcli connection up eth0</code>: Reactivates the eth0 network interface.</li>
      <li><code>sudo nmcli connection show</code>: Lists known connection profiles.</li>
      <li><code>sudo nmcli connection up "System eth0"</code>: Variant to force the connection.</li>
      <li><code>sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager</code>: Restarts the network management service.</li>
      <li><code>sudo dnf install nano -y</code>: Installs the nano text editor.</li>
      <li><code>sudo nmtui</code>: Semi-graphical interface for configuring the static IP.</li>
      <li><code>ping 192.168.1.254</code>: Tests connectivity with the gateway.</li>
      <li><code>ping 192.168.1.64</code>: Tests connectivity with the host PC.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Issabel Installation and Verification</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "core show version"</code>: Displays the Asterisk version.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show endpoints"</code>: Displays the list of PJSIP extensions.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show endpoint 1003"</code>: Displays details of extension 1003.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show transports"</code>: Verifies that UDP transport is active.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show aors"</code>: Displays AOR objects.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. TCP/UDP Conflict Diagnosis</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rvvv</code>: Opens the Asterisk console in verbose mode.</li>
      <li><code>pjsip send unregister 1003</code>: Forces disconnection of extension 1003.</li>
      <li><code>pjsip reload</code>: Reloads the PJSIP configuration.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip qualify 1003"</code>: Manually tests the reachability of extension 1003.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show endpoint 1003"</code>: Verifies that the displayed transport is indeed UDP.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Bash Script Development and Testing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>sudo nano /usr/local/bin/test_telegram.sh</code>: Creates the first test script.</li>
      <li><code>sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/test_telegram.sh</code>: Makes the script executable.</li>
      <li><code>sudo /usr/local/bin/test_telegram.sh</code>: Runs the test script.</li>
      <li><code>sudo nano /usr/local/bin/send_telegram_alert.sh</code>: Creates the final script.</li>
      <li><code>sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/send_telegram_alert.sh</code>: Makes the final script executable.</li>
      <li><code>sudo /usr/local/bin/send_telegram_alert.sh "1001" "1003"</code>: Manual test of the final script.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Asterisk Dialplan Integration</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>sudo nano /etc/asterisk/extensions_custom.conf</code>: Edits the custom configuration file.</li>
      <li><code>sudo cat /etc/asterisk/extensions_custom.conf</code>: Verifies the file content.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "dialplan reload"</code>: Reloads the dialplan.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "dialplan show telegram-alert"</code>: Verifies that the <code>[telegram-alert]</code> context is correctly interpreted.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "dialplan show from-internal-custom"</code>: Verifies that code <code>9999</code> is correctly taken into account.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6. Final Verification and Testing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rvvv</code>: Real-time log monitoring during the call.</li>
      <li><code>sudo asterisk -rx "pjsip show endpoints"</code>: Final verification that extensions are <code>Reachable</code>.</li>
      <li><code>sudo tail -f /var/log/messages</code>: System log monitoring.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>7. Maintenance and Debugging</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>sudo systemctl status asterisk</code>: Verifies that the Asterisk service is running.</li>
      <li><code>sudo systemctl restart asterisk</code>: Completely restarts the Asterisk service.</li>
      <li><code>sudo systemctl status firewalld</code>: Checks the firewall status.</li>
      <li><code>sudo systemctl stop firewalld</code>: Temporarily disables the firewall.</li>
      <li><code>sudo systemctl disable firewalld</code>: Disables the firewall at boot.</li>
      <li><code>sudo reboot</code>: Restarts the VM after a major network configuration.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Complete Project Gallery</h2>
    <p>Find below all the screenshots documenting each step of the project. Click on an image to enlarge it.</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-8" id="issabel-gallery">
      {Object.entries(cloudinaryImages['issabel-telegram'] || {})
        .filter(([key, url]) => {
          // Exclude logo, architecture and videos (already displayed above)
          if (['logo', 'architecture'].includes(key)) return false;
          if (url.includes('/video/upload/')) return false;
          // Only keep valid image extensions
          const validExtensions = ['.png', '.jpg', '.jpeg', '.webp', '.svg', '.gif'];
          return validExtensions.some((ext) => url.toLowerCase().endsWith(ext));
        })
        .map(([key, url]) => (
          <div
            class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border glass cursor-pointer hover-lift gallery-item"
            style="border-color: var(--glass-border);"
            data-lightbox-src={url}
          >
            <img
              src={url}
              alt={key}
              class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <div class="p-3">
              <p class="font-mono text-[10px]" style="color: var(--text-muted);">{key}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
    </div>

    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        function initGallery() {
          const galleryItems = document.querySelectorAll('#issabel-gallery .gallery-item');
          if (galleryItems.length === 0) {
            console.warn('Issabel Gallery: no elements found.');
            return;
          }
          galleryItems.forEach(function (item) {
            item.addEventListener('click', function () {
              const src = item.getAttribute('data-lightbox-src');
              if (!src) return;

              // Try to use the global function if it exists
              if (typeof window.openLightbox === 'function') {
                window.openLightbox(src);
                return;
              }

              // Fallback: manual overlay
              const overlay = document.createElement('div');
              overlay.style.cssText =
                'position:fixed;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.9);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;z-index:9999;cursor:pointer;';
              const img = document.createElement('img');
              img.src = src;
              img.style.cssText = 'max-width:90%;max-height:90%;object-fit:contain;border-radius:8px;';
              overlay.appendChild(img);
              overlay.addEventListener('click', function () {
                overlay.remove();
              });
              document.body.appendChild(overlay);
            });
          });
          console.log('Issabel Gallery: ' + galleryItems.length + ' images initialized.');
        }

        // Wait for the DOM to be ready
        if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
          document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', initGallery);
        } else {
          initGallery();
        }
      })();
    </script>
  </ProjectLayout>
</BaseLayout>